6"] "\n" [:option {:value "19009100"} "2020"] "\n" [:option {:value "2012200"} "2024"]] [:script {} "$('#other_games_select').change(function(){location.href='/results/' + $('#other_games_select').val();})"]] " | " [:a {:href "/event_names/311"} "Event type"]] [:h1 {:class "event_title"} "Individual All-Around, Men" [:small {}]] [:table {:class "biodata"} [:tbody {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Date"] [:td {} "20 – 24 July 1980"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Status"] [:td {} "Olympic"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/588"} "Dvorets sporta, Tsentralny stadion imeni V. I. Lenina, Moskva"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/70455"} "65 from 14 countries"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "The format was the same as in 1976. The team all-around was contested first, with the top 36 finishers from that event advancing to the individual all-around final. However, to prevent individual nations from dominating with over-representation, no more than three gymnasts from any one country could advance to the individual final. The total score consisted of 50% of the team all-around score and the performances on all six apparatuses in the individual final. Soviet " [:a {:href "/athletes/31235"} "Nikolay Andrianov"] " was the defending champion and had won the 1978 World Championships, but in 1979, his teammate, " [:a {:href "/athletes/31244"} "Aleksandr Dityatin"] " won the Worlds, followed by American " [:a {:href "/athletes/31451"} "Kurt Thomas"] ". Thomas could not compete, done in by " [:a {:href "/athletes/1200055"} "Jimmy Carter"] " and the American-led boycott. In his absence this event came down to a battle between Dityatin and Andrianov, with Dityatin winning gold and Andrianov close behind for silver, the bronze going to Bulgarian " [:a {:href "/athletes/29478"} "Stoyan Delchev"] ". Delchev’s medal was the first by a Bulgarian gymnast at the Olympics since a bronze by " [:a {:href "/athletes/29493"} "Velik Kapsazov"] " on rings in 1960."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Dityatin would go on to win eight medals in Moskva, breaking the record for the most medals at a single Olympics, previously seven held by seven Olympians. Eight remains the record through 2012, having been tied by " [:a {:href "/athletes/93860"} "Michael Phelps"] " in 2004 and 2008. Andrianov had won seven medals in 1976, and would add five in 1980, ending his Olympic career with 15 medals, a record for men at the Olympics until broken by Phelps in 2008."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Points"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "64"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/31244"} "Aleksandr Dityatin"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/URS"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/URS.png"}] "URS"]] [:td {} "118.650"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {:class "bib"} "62"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/31235"} "Nikolay Andrianov"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/URS"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/URS.png"}] "URS"]] [:td {} "118.225"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Silver"} "Silver"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "3"] [:td {:class "bib"} "4"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/29478"} "Stoyan Delchev"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/BUL"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/BUL.png"}] "BUL"]] [:td {} "118.000"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Bronze"} "Bronze"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "4"] [:td {:class "bib"} "68"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/31273"} "Aleksandr Tkachov"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/URS"} [:img {:style "